            "providerContent": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Alberto builds agents with a practical, measured approach:\n\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** His site's agent records eval cases against a real model and replays them in CI with no model or network. A stale recording or an off-topic answer fails the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Human in the loop, graded autonomy:** His fraud detection agent recommends approve, reject or request-info, and a human admin decides. It auto-approves only on evidence verified in code, and web sources can hold a decision back but never approve it.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** The site's agent has a per-request token budget, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show mean tokens, latency and cost per case. ([guardrails](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public#guardrails))\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run appends its metrics to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show against the previous run. ([eval history](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/history.jsonl))\n\nIn practice, the fraud detection agent cut approved fraud from 54% to 8% over twelve iterations on a local model. Switching to Claude Haiku 5.5 for deployment gave 86% accuracy at about $0.002 per review. You can see the full [eval report](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/agent/evals/report.md) for this site's agent."
              }
            ]
          }
        }
