              "type": "response_end",
              "response": {
                "text": "Alberto builds agents with a practical, measured approach:\n\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** His site's agent records eval cases against a real model and replays them in CI with no network, so stale recordings or off-topic leaks fail the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy with a human in the loop:** In his fraud detection agent, the agent recommends and a human admin decides. It auto-approves only on evidence verified in code.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** The site's agent has a per-request token budget, per-IP rate limits and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show mean tokens, latency and cost per case.\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run is appended to a history, so changes to prompts, tools or content show up against the previous run.\n\nExample result: on his fraud detection agent's 31 offline eval cases, switching from a local 9B model to Claude Haiku 5.5 raised accuracy from 71% to 86% and cut fraud approved without review from 8% to 1%, at about $0.002 per review.\n\nThe stack for these agents includes TypeScript, PostgreSQL, GCP, Claude and MCP. Would you like details on any of these projects?",
