                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Alberto builds agents with a few consistent practices:\n\n- **Offline evals before shipping:** On this site, the agent records eval cases against a real model and replays them in CI with no network. A stale recording or an off-topic answer fails the build. ([ADR 0001](https://github.com/acarbonellb/acb-landing-public/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-agent-evals-record-replay.md))\n- **Graded autonomy with a human in the loop:** His fraud detection agent recommends approve, reject, or request-info, and a human admin decides. It auto-approves only on evidence verified in code.\n- **Cost and limits tracked:** This site's agent has a per-request token budget, per-IP rate limits, and a monthly spend cap. Eval reports show tokens, latency, and cost per case.\n- **Changes measured run over run:** Each eval run is appended to a history, so changes to prompts, tools, or content show up against the previous run.\n\nFor example, on the fraud detection agent (31 eval cases), he moved from a local 9B model to Claude Haiku 5.5. Accuracy rose from 71% to 86%, and fraud approved without a human fell from 8% to 1%, at about $0.002 per review. Its stack is TypeScript, PostgreSQL, and GCP."
